<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The vectored airship with hull aerodynamics. Same vehicle as
  airship_jaxa_rigid, which this merges: only the fluid the hull
  moves through is added, so a run against either model is the same control
  code on two plants.

  Quadratic drag and rotational damping, no added mass. The added-mass terms
  this plugin also accepts are left out on purpose: it applies them as an
  explicit force, and at an airship's ratios (the hull's added sway mass is of
  the order of its dry mass) that diverges within a fraction of a second. Added
  mass belongs in the link's <fluid_added_mass>, which the physics engine folds
  into the spatial inertia: airship_jaxa_base_am carries it, and
  airship_jaxa_am_fin_drag has both.

  The dimensionless coefficients are the airship-control-design reference set
  (c_d 0.08906 axial, c_lat 3.08324 lateral, c_lp_lin 0.04 and c_lp_quad 0.18
  roll, c_mq = c_nr 1.2 pitch and yaw) on an 8.3534 x 2.3693 m hull. The fluid
  is air at rho = 1.225 kg/m^3 despite the plugin's name. On this hull,
  airship_jaxa_base_rigid's 14.00 x 4.00 m spheroid, the frontal reference
  radius scales with the half-height to 1.85709 m (A = 10.8346 m^2); the
  lateral coefficient is crossflow drag, which scales with the planform, so it
  also takes the fineness ratio (3.5 / 3.5257, x 0.9927); the axial one takes
  Hoerner's fineness dependence of a streamlined body's frontal drag (x
  0.9970); and the rotational scale is 0.5 * rho * S * L^3 * 0.003438 with
  S = 142.7210 m^2 and L = 14.0000 m, giving 824.6771 N m. Translational terms
  are then -0.5 * rho * A * c times those factors, rotational terms
  -824.6771 * c.

  The plugin applies its wrench at the link origin (gz::sim::Link has an
  offset form, but Hydrodynamics calls the two-argument AddWorldWrench, which
  the header documents as acting at the link's origin). It reads the velocity
  there too. So the origin, not the hull axis and not the center of mass, is
  the point this whole coefficient set is written about, and it sits 3 m below
  the axis the drag acts on. About a point off the axis the set is not
  diagonal, and not in one way but two, so six cross terms.

  Two conditions make the plugin's wrench the hull's. The force it applies has
  to be the force on the hull, which is set by the velocity at the axis and
  not at the origin; and the couple has to be d x F for d = (0, 0, 3), the
  offset between the two points. The center of mass appears in neither, which
  is the check on the algebra: a derivation that only matches the moment about
  the center of mass satisfies the second condition and quietly leaves the
  first one broken, because the force error's own lever hides it there.

  So each in-plane axis needs four coefficients where the plugin had one: the
  drag itself, its rate term on the force, its arm on the couple, and that
  arm's rate term. The arm pair is what stops forward flight pitching the nose
  down. The rate terms are what stop the hull rolling itself apart: without
  both of them the roll moment per unit rate about the centre of mass is
  -32.99 + 78.69 |v| N m/(rad/s), which stops damping above 0.42 m/s of
  sideslip (on the earlier hull's coefficients that settled into a 6 deg limit
  cycle), and without kPabsV alone it is -32.99 + 193.30 |v|, which stops
  damping above 0.17 m/s.

  One term of the shift has no home in this plugin's grammar, a squared rate
  that follows the sign of the velocity rather than of the rate. At the center
  of mass it is worth 16.5 N of sway and 33.9 N m of roll at 0.3 rad/s against
  2889 N m/rad of restoring, and it falls away with the rate. The expansion
  behind all of it holds while the rate-induced velocity stays under the
  velocity itself. Past that, which is near-still air, these terms overshoot
  rather than reverse: in still air the hull's own contribution to roll damping
  reads 199.1 N m/(rad/s)^2 where it should read 177.4, so the total with
  kPabsP comes out 347.5 against 325.9, 7 % high and on the safe side. The
  rotational damping is a pure couple, equal about every point and untouched by
  any of this.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="airship_jaxa_drag">
    <include merge="true">
      <uri>model://airship_jaxa_rigid</uri>
    </include>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-hydrodynamics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Hydrodynamics">
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <!-- Quadratic drag [N/(m/s)^2] -->
      <xUabsU>-0.5893</xUabsU>
      <yVabsV>-20.3120</yVabsV>
      <zWabsW>-20.3120</zWabsW>
      <!-- Rotational damping [N m/(rad/s)^2], with the linear roll term the
           very lightly damped roll mode needs [N m/(rad/s)] -->
      <kP>-32.9871</kP>
      <kPabsP>-148.4419</kPabsP>
      <mQabsQ>-989.6125</mQabsQ>
      <nRabsR>-989.6125</nRabsR>
      <!-- The same two drags written about the link origin, h = 3 m below
           the hull axis they act on. Surge and pitch, from u_hull = u + h q
           and M_y = +h F_x; sway and roll, from v_hull = v - h p and
           M_x = -h F_y. Both signs follow from d x F for d = (0, 0, h), and
           the leading minus on yPabsV and kVabsV is that, not a typo.
             xQabsU = 2h  * xUabsU   yPabsV = -2h  * yVabsV   [N/(rad/s)/(m/s)]
             mUabsU =  h  * xUabsU   kVabsV =  -h  * yVabsV   [N m/(m/s)^2]
             mQabsU = 2h^2* xUabsU   kPabsV = 2h^2 * yVabsV   [N m/(rad/s)/(m/s)]
           Heave has no row of its own: its line of action runs through both
           points, and no rate changes it, because d is along it. -->
      <xQabsU>-3.5356</xQabsU>
      <yPabsV>121.8721</yPabsV>
      <mUabsU>-1.7678</mUabsU>
      <mQabsU>-10.6069</mQabsU>
      <kVabsV>60.9361</kVabsV>
      <kPabsV>-365.6163</kPabsV>
      <!-- Inert while no xDot* term is set, and kept for when one is: it is
           also what tells the plugin to stand back if the link ever gains a
           <fluid_added_mass>, which it would otherwise double-count. -->
      <disable_added_mass>true</disable_added_mass>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
